<h2>The case below</h2>
<p>The husband sued for divorce before the Additional Family Court-II, Dhanbad (Original Suit No. 536 of 2021), alleging that the wife was moody, refused intimacy, confined herself to her room, quarrelled, misbehaved at family events and left the matrimonial home in June 2021, all of it aggravated — he said — by her mother&rsquo;s interference. The wife denied cruelty and made her own allegations of dowry demands, surveillance through CCTV and spy software, and assault during pregnancy. She maintained throughout that she wanted the marriage to continue. The Family Court dismissed the suit on 17 January 2025, and the husband appealed.</p>

<h2>Where the husband&rsquo;s case failed</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><h3>Hearsay support</h3><p>The husband&rsquo;s corroborating witness was his brother, who testified only to what he had been told. Hearsay cannot prove cruelty, which must be established by witnesses with direct knowledge or by reliable documents.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h3>Self-contradiction</h3><p>The husband admitted in cross-examination that the wife attended family functions, and stated that he himself refused to take her back — admissions that cut against both cruelty and the narrative of desertion by the wife.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h3>Consistent respondent</h3><p>The wife&rsquo;s evidence was consistent and corroborated by her mother, and her stated willingness to resume cohabitation stood in contrast to the husband&rsquo;s stand that he did not wish to keep her.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The pen drive problem</h2>
<p>The husband&rsquo;s central attack on appeal was that the Family Court had ignored electronic evidence contained in a pen drive. The Division Bench rejected the argument at the threshold: the pen drive was never admitted into evidence, the wife was not supplied a copy despite repeated opportunities, and the record lacked the certification and authentication that electronic evidence demands — a requirement rooted in Section 65B of the Evidence Act and carried forward under the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023, as explained in <em>Arjun Panditrao Khotkar</em>. The Court put it crisply: the mere production of electronic material does not, by itself, establish the truth of its contents.</p>

<div class="compare">
<div class="col old"><h3>Producing a device</h3><p>Handing a pen drive, phone or recording to the court proves nothing. Without admission into evidence, supply to the other side and statutory certification, it is inert material.</p></div>
<div class="arrow">→</div>
<div class="col new"><h3>Proving a record</h3><p>Electronic evidence must be exhibited, certified and authenticated, with the opposite party given the chance to test it. Only then can its contents be weighed.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The standard the Bench applied</h2>
<p>The Bench reiterated that an allegation of cruelty cannot be accepted merely because the matrimonial relationship has become strained, and that the jurisdiction to dissolve a marriage on the ground of cruelty is exercised only upon proof of the statutory ground. On a full reappraisal it found no perversity in the trial court&rsquo;s conclusions, and in paragraph 69 recorded that the husband had failed to establish cruelty on the part of the wife. The appeal was dismissed and the decree affirmed.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>For litigants, the practical message is sobering: a divorce suit is won on admissible proof, not on the volume of grievances. WhatsApp exports, recordings and pen drives are now routine in matrimonial trials, and they are just as routinely wasted for want of certification, supply to the other side and proper exhibition. Electronic material should be planned as evidence from the day the petition is drafted.</p>
</div>
